<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?> 

<section xml:id="section_1_1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Preliminary: Introduction to the six-step method</title>

<p>
  <term>Statistics</term> is a discipline that guides researchers in 
  collecting, exploring, and drawing conclusions from data.  This course 
  will demonstrate the statistical investigation process for a variety of 
  fields of application.
</p>

<subsection> 
  <title>The six steps of a statistical investigation</title>
  <p>
    The <term>six steps of a statistical investigation</term> is a framework 
    for learning from data.  The six steps are a guide for collecting and 
    interpreting data in keeping with the scientific method. When research 
    questions are investigated with the six-step method, conclusions are 
    supported by systematic study rather than individual experiences. The six 
    steps are described in the table below.
  </p>
  <p>
    <dl>
      <li>
        <title>1. Ask a research question.</title>
        <p>
          Confirm the question can be addressed by collecting data. Typical 
          questions involve comparing groups, asking how two characteristics 
          are related, or surveying people's opinions.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>2. Design a study and collect data.</title>
        <p>
          Plan how to select the people or objects to be studied, decide how to 
          gather the relevant data, and carry out the data collection in a careful, 
          systematic manner.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>3. Explore the data.</title>
        <p>
          Look for patterns that relate to the research question or raise additional 
          questions.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>4. Draw inferences beyond the data.</title>
        <p>
          Determine whether any findings in the data reflect a genuine tendency and 
          estimate the strength of that tendency.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>5. Formulate conclusions</title>
        <p>
          Consider whether inferences made in step 4 can be generalized to processes 
          or groups beyond those represented in the study. Identify whether a 
          cause-and-effect conclusion is warranted.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>6. Look back and ahead.</title>
        <p>
          Point out limitations of the study and suggest new studies to build on the findings.
        </p>
      </li>
    </dl>
  </p>

<activity>
 <title> Asking a research question</title>
<statement>
<p>State a research question you would be interested in answering.</p>
</statement>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title> What affects the choice to become an organ donor?</title>
<p>Researchers <url href="https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=1324774">
  Johnson and Goldstein</url> asked 161 volunteer participants to imagine moving to 
  a new state and applying for a driver's license. One part of the application asked 
  whether the participants would agree to be an organ donor. Participants were presented 
  with one of three different forms of the question:
</p>
<p>
  <ul>
    <li> You will not be a donor unless you opt in.</li>
    <li> You will be a donor unless you opt out.</li>
    <li> Choose whether or not to be a donor (neutral).</li>
  </ul>
</p>

<p></p>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title> Basic terminology</title>
  <p>Some basic terminology of data is required. </p>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Data</term> are the values measured or categories recorded on 
          individual objects of interest.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The <term>observational unit</term> is the individual object on which 
          data are recorded.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          A <term>variable</term> is a characteristic being recorded about the 
          observational units. 
        </p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                A <term>quantitative variable</term> has numeric values for which 
                ordinary arithmetic (addition, multiplication) makes sense. 
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                A <term>categorical variable</term> places observational units 
                into categories.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>      
      <li>
        <p>
          A <term>distribution</term> is the pattern of outcomes of a variable.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>      
  </p>

  <remark>
    <p>
      The best way to determine if a variable is quantitative or categorical is to ask the 
      question, <q>Can I take the average of this? And would that make sense?</q> For example, 
      I could ask a group of people to tell me what ZIP code they currently live in, and I 
      could technically take the average of those numbers, but it wouldn't make sense to do 
      so. That is because ZIP codes are categorical.
    </p>
  </remark>



<activity>
 <title> Study design elements</title>
<statement>
<p>For the research question you stated at the beginning of the section, identify the 
  observational units and variable(s) you would use,  and describe how you would collect 
  the data. For each variable, indicate whether the variable would be quantitative or 
  categorical.</p>
</statement>
</activity>

</subsection>

</section>